## Safe Access with .get()

When you need a specific item from a Vec, `.get(index)` lets you ask safely. If the index exists, you get `Some(value)`. If it doesn't, you get `None` — no crashes.

### How .get() Works

```xiom
let scores: Vec[Int] = Vec[Int].new();
scores.push(95);
scores.push(82);
scores.push(74);

let first = scores.get(0);
```

`scores.get(0)` returns `Some(95)` — the first element exists.

### What Happens Out of Bounds?

```xiom
let missing = scores.get(100);
```

`scores.get(100)` returns `None` — there's no element at index 100. No crash, no panic, just a safe `None`.

### Using .len() Together with .get()

`.len()` tells you how many items exist, so you can check before accessing:

```xiom
if index < scores.len() {
  match scores.get(index) {
    Some(val) -> io.println(val);
    None -> io.println("unreachable!");
  }
}
else {
  io.println("Index out of bounds");
}
```

### Full Program

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  let items: Vec[Str] = Vec[Str].new();
  items.push("apple");
  items.push("banana");
  items.push("cherry");

  match items.get(0) {
    Some(f) -> io.println(f);
    None -> io.println("no item");
  }

  match items.get(5) {
    Some(f) -> io.println(f);
    None -> io.println("no item");
  }

  io.println(items.len());
  return 0;
}
```

